<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erich Mühsam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Erich Mühsam was a German anarchist writer, poet and satirist. He helped proclaim the Bavarian Soviet Republic in April 1919 and was sentenced to fifteen years' imprisonment, serving until an amnesty in 1924. Arrested by the Nazis after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> fire in 1933, he was murdered in Oranienburg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camp</a> in July 1934.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erich-muhsam.jpg" alt="Erich Mühsam" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1981-003-08,_Erich_M%C3%BChsam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Erich Mühsam was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 6 April 1878, the third child of Siegfried Seligmann Mühsam, a Jewish pharmacist, and his wife Rosalie. The family soon moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, where he attended the Katharineum-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a>, a school known for harsh discipline. In January 1896 he wrote an anonymous piece denouncing a teacher, and when his authorship was discovered he was expelled for socialist activities. He finished school in Parchim. His father made him study pharmacy, which he abandoned for a literary life, and in 1900 he moved to Berlin, where he joined the communal group Neue Gemeinschaft and met the anarchist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-landauer">Gustav Landauer</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1904 Mühsam withdrew to an artists' commune in Ascona and began writing plays, the first being Die Hochstapler. He edited anarchist journals, was under constant police surveillance, and in 1908 moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, where he wrote cabaret songs that became among his best-known work. In 1911 he founded the newspaper Kain, which ridiculed the state and opposed capital punishment and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> censorship.</p><p>In 1915 he married Kreszentia Elfinger. Early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he wrote in support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, but renounced that position by the end of 1914 and later took part in strikes against the war. He was arrested in April 1918 and held until 3 November 1918.</p><p>In the revolution that followed Mühsam refused a ministry offered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-eisner">Kurt Eisner</a> and worked with Landauer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernst-toller">Ernst Toller</a> and others for workers' councils. After Eisner's assassination the Bavarian Soviet Republic was proclaimed, and it lasted only six days before communists led by Eugen Levine overthrew it. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, Landauer was killed and Mühsam was arrested and sentenced to fifteen years in fortress confinement. In prison he wrote the play Judas and many poems. After his release under the amnesty of 1924 he founded the journal Fanal in 1926 and satirised the Nazis, notably in the short story Die Affenschande.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mühsam was arrested without charge on 28 February 1933, hours after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> fire, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Joseph Goebbels</a> named him among Jewish subversives. Over the next seventeen months he was held at Sonnenburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and Oranienburg camps and repeatedly beaten and tortured. On the night of 9 July 1934 he was killed, and his body was found hanging in a latrine the next morning. An official Nazi report of 11 July called it suicide in protective custody; his widow, Zenzl, said the body bore signs of strangulation. Later accounts named the former Dachau commander Theodor Eicke as the murderer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Erich Mühsam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 6-7 April 1919 a council republic was proclaimed by writers and anarchists, including Ernst Toller, <b>Erich Mühsam</b> and Gustav Landauer.</blockquote><a href="/state/bavarian-soviet-republic">Bavarian Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Oranienburg concentration camp, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1914" aria-label="Berlin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4850 352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Erich Mühsam"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5300 622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1914">What was here in 1914? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
